================================================================================
STAGE 1 FINAL DECISION: FROST FEATURE
================================================================================
VERDICT: NO-GO.

NULL OUTCOME APPLIED: THE AVAILABLE INSTRUMENTS CANNOT SEE IT.

EVIDENCE:
1. Station Network Geometry: All available synoptic stations occupy the topographically neutral center of the landscape (TPI span 18.59 m, between 28th and 84th percentiles), with exactly ZERO stations located in terrain hollows (TPI < -50 m). The station network cannot observe nocturnal pooling.
2. Canopy Decoupling: In the escarpment canyons where terrain relief is strongest, closed forest canopy insulates the sub-canopy air, rendering satellite thermal radiometry (MODIS 1km LST) blind to surface drainage.
3. Satellite Scale Mismatch: In open-terrain plateau valleys, when broad elevation is controlled, the partial correlation between microtopographic position and residual LST is statistically indistinguishable from zero (r_partial = -0.069 decoupled vs -0.034 coupled, Welch's t = -0.73, p = 0.47). A 1 km satellite pixel averages over local drainage channels, smoothing out the phenomenon.
4. DEM Noise Floor: 87.12% of domain villages have |TPI| < 27.14 m, placing them below the representation uncertainty between the 250m DEM and true terrain elevation.

The physical mechanism of cold-air drainage is not disproven in nature, but because the available instruments (0.25° ERA5, 5 neutral synoptic stations, and 1km MODIS LST) cannot observe, calibrate, or validate it, building a downscaled pool-filling, shoreline, or frost-dose product would be speculative and unscientific. The feature is formally terminated.
